  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1222 PM CDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nMidday surface analysis indicated high pressure centered over\nthe eastern Great Lakes with ridging extending west into\nnorthern and central IL. Aloft, water vapor imagery showed a mid\nlevel shortwave tracking northeast near the MO bootheel. This\nsystem will provide forcing for isolated/scattered showers and\npossibly a few storms tonight for parts of southeast to east-\ncentral IL.\n\nThe next system of interest is an area of low pressure currently\nover SD. As this shifts northeast on Saturday, it will push a\ncold front into IA. Ahead of the front, thermal ridging brings\nanother warming trend with highs the upper 80s to around 90.\nThe front looks to stall in a west-east fashion over central IL\non Sunday, resulting in a large temperature gradient with highs\nin the upper 70s over the far northern CWA, to the lower 90s\nsouth of I-70. The forecast does carry low (20-30%) PoPs in the\nvicinity of the front, however, limited upper level support and\nwarm temperatures aloft should act to limit shower and storm\ncoverage.\n\nA more active period of weather is projected across the Midwest\nfor much of next week. A strong upper level ridge will be\nanchored over the lower Mississippi River Valley for much of the\nweek. Farther north, the northern branch of the upper jet will\nsupply a series of disturbances to the northern Plains and upper\nMidwest. This is forecast to produce numerous rounds of \nthunderstorms near or just to the north of central IL for \nseveral days, most likely Tuesday through Thursday. While \nmoisture and upper level dynamics are projected to be not as \nstrong as what we saw during the \"ring of fire\" pattern around a\nmonth ago, it appears there will be ingredients present for a \nfew rounds of locally heavy rain and potential severe weather, \nthough ML/AI severe outlooks generally point to a 5% or less \ndaily risk. Temperature trends will be closely tied to the \nposition of the front and associated convectively cooled \noutflow. This will likely result in a large north to south \ntemperature gradient across the region, with areas south of the \nfront flirting with daily afternoon heat indices near 100 \ndegrees.",
